From: Paul Scullion <p.scullion@keynvor-morlift.example> (Keynvor Morlift)
To: Phil Walker <p.walker@pharos-offshore.example> (Pharos Offshore Group)
Date: not stated
Subject: Viking Link - KML Defence and Counterclaim

KML denies the value claimed. The sum that ought to have been invoiced is £274,742.05 (ex VAT), on the basis of the Revised Purchase Order.

The trenching work was to take 10 days. This was calculated using a progress rate of 50m/hr (which was significantly lower than the 250-300m/hr which the Claimant represented was possible), allowing 3 days mobilisation and 1 day demobilisation plus an additional margin of about 1.3 days. The mobilisation date was 9 June 2022; the UTV-670 did not arrive until 20 June 2022.

The reference to weather downtime was deleted from the Revised Purchase Order, so the risk of waiting on weather lies with Pharos.

The UTV-670 was not fit for its intended purpose: throughout the works it suffered various issues including sensor issues, repeated failure to maintain pressure, inability to maintain traction, hose failures and HPU failures. On average for every 1.6 hours that the UTV-670 worked 1 hour of breakdown/maintenance time was incurred. The lay-flat hoses were prone to sink when filled with water, to strain the rigging securing the buoys used to keep them afloat, and to kink and become entangled with the guideline and the communication umbilical; appropriate floating hoses should have been deployed. Pharos should have advised that the UTV-670 be deployed first to work in the offshore area.

KML counterclaims between £1,217,806.86 and £1,434,049.50 for additional vessel/spread hire and harbour fees by way of damages and/or liquidated damages of £185,280, and will set these off against any sum found due.

